    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Assessment of the Offer\n\nThis is a **solid baseline offer**, but it leaves substantial money on the table for a passive candidate. \n\n* **Leveling:** Meta IC6 (Staff PM) to Google L6 (Senior PM / Group PM) is an appropriate lateral match.\n* **Cash Compensation:** Your base at Google ($235,000) is slightly lower than your current Meta base ($240,000), though your target cash is higher due to Google's 20% bonus target ($282,000 total cash vs. $276,000 at Meta).\n* **Base Salary Band:** With a posted range of $183,000 to $271,000, $235,000 sits around the midpoint.\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** At **$0**, it fails to account for the risk and unvested Meta equity you are walking away from.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Real Compensation Math (Front-Loaded Vesting)\n\nGoogle typically uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** for initial grants (33% Year 1, 33% Year 2, 22% Year 3, 12% Year 4). \n\n| Year | Base Salary | Target Bonus (20%) | GSU Vesting (33/33/22/12) | Total Compensation (Pre-Refresher) | Meta Baseline (Current) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $356,400 | **~$638,400** | $526,000 |\n| **Year 2** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $356,400 | **~$638,400** | $526,000+ |\n| **Year 3** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $237,600 | **~$519,600** | $526,000+ |\n| **Year 4** | $235,000 | $47,000 | $129,600 | **~$411,600** | $526,000+ |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n1. **The Short-Term Upside:** In Years 1 and 2, your total compensation increases by **~$112,000/year (~21%)** compared to Meta.\n2. **The \"Cliff\" in Years 3 & 4:** Total comp drops significantly after Year 2. Annual refresh grants (which vest evenly at 25%/year over 4 years) are meant to fill this gap, but they are performance-dependent and not contractually guaranteed.\n3. **Flat 4-Year Average:** If viewed as a flat 4-year package, the average is **$552,000/year**, which is only ~5% above your current Meta earnings.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Negotiate.** \n\nYou have high leverage in this scenario:\n* Google initiated contact.\n* You are satisfied in your current role at Meta.\n* Your current compensation ($526k) is strong.\n* You have roughly $200,000 in unvested Meta RSUs vesting over the next 12 months.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For & Target Numbers\n\nPresent a counter-offer targeting the upper quartile for an L6 PM in the Bay Area:\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Counter-Proposal | Target Compromise |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000** | $1,150,000 \u2013 $1,200,000 |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$80,000** | $50,000 \u2013 $75,000 |\n\n* **Base Salary ($255,000):** Brings you above your current Meta base ($240,000) and into the upper half of the $183k\u2013$271k band.\n* **Equity ($1,250,000):** Increases your 4-year average equity from $270k/yr to $312.5k/yr, providing greater protection against the Year 3/4 drop.\n* **Sign-On Bonus ($80,000):** Offsets leaving your near-term unvested Meta equity cliff and the transition risk.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nFrame the negotiation positively while emphasizing that leaving a stable, high-performing situation at Meta requires a stronger financial case.\n\n#### Sample Script / Email Framework:\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I enjoyed meeting the team and am excited about the impact of this L6 scope.*\n> \n> *Because Google reached out while I am well-established and happy at Meta, making a move needs to be compelling across the entire compensation structure. Looking at the numbers:*\n> \n> 1. *My current base salary at Meta is $240,000. I would like to see the base salary at **$255,000**, which is within the posted range for this role.*\n> 2. *To bridge the equity I am walking away from over the coming year (around $200k vesting) and account for the transition, I am asking for a sign-on bonus of **$80,000**.*\n> 3. *To ensure the long-term package remains competitive across all four years, I would like to increase the initial equity grant to **$1,250,000**.*\n> \n> *If we can reach these terms, I am prepared to sign and begin planning my start date.\"*\n\n### Next Steps:\n1. **Confirm the vesting schedule in writing:** Ensure your offer details whether it is the 33/33/22/12 model or a flat 25%/year schedule.\n2. **Anchor high and hold firm:** If Google pushes back on base salary, shift the focus toward a larger sign-on bonus and increased equity grant size, where recruiters typically have wider discretion.",
